01:49:35.240 --> 01:49:42.020
As we go through our very brief introduction to&nbsp;
modeling data and are another common procedure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:42.020 --> 01:49:47.540
that we might want to look at briefly, is called&nbsp;
principal components. And the idea here is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:47.540 --> 01:49:56.420
in certain situations, less is more. That is less&nbsp;
noise, and fewer unhelpful variables in your data&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:56.420 --> 01:50:03.530
can translate to more meaning and that's why&nbsp;
After In any case, now, this approach is also&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:03.530 --> 01:50:10.250
known as dimensionality reduction. And I like to&nbsp;
think of it by an analogy, you look at this photo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:10.250 --> 01:50:15.410
and what you see are these big black outlines of&nbsp;
people, you can tell basically how tall they are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:15.410 --> 01:50:18.530
what they're wearing, where they're going. And&nbsp;
it takes a moment to realize that you're actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:18.530 --> 01:50:22.910
looking at a photograph that goes straight down.&nbsp;
And you can see the people there on the bottom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:22.910 --> 01:50:27.500
and you're looking at their shadows. And we're&nbsp;
trying to do a similar thing. Even though these&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:27.500 --> 01:50:31.340
are shadows, you can still tell a lot about&nbsp;
the people, people are three dimensional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:31.340 --> 01:50:37.940
shadows are two dimensional, but we've retained&nbsp;
almost all the important information. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:37.940 --> 01:50:42.710
want to do this with data, the most common&nbsp;
method is called principal component analysis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:42.710 --> 01:50:50.780
or PCA. And let me give you an example of the&nbsp;
steps metaphorically in PCA. You begin with two&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:50.780 --> 01:50:56.480
variables. And so here's a scatterplot, we've got&nbsp;
x across the bottom y decide, and this is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:56.480 --> 01:51:02.390
artificial data. And you can see that there's a&nbsp;
strong linear association between these two. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:02.390 --> 01:51:06.440
what we're going to do is we're going to draw&nbsp;
a regression line through the data set, and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:06.440 --> 01:51:12.490
know, it's there about 45 degrees. And then we're&nbsp;
going to measure the perpendicular distance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:12.490 --> 01:51:17.530
each data point to the regression line. Now, not&nbsp;
the vertical distance, that's what we would do if&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:17.530 --> 01:51:22.990
we were looking for regression residuals, but the&nbsp;
perpendicular distance. And that's what those red&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:22.990 --> 01:51:28.210
lines are, then what we're going to do is we're&nbsp;
going to collapse the data by sliding each point&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:28.210 --> 01:51:33.850
down the red line to the regression line. And&nbsp;
that's what we have there. And then finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:33.850 --> 01:51:41.620
we have the option of rotating it. So it's not on&nbsp;
diagonal anymore, but it's flat. And that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:41.620 --> 01:51:48.280
is the PC the principal component. Now, let's&nbsp;
recap what we've accomplished here, we went from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:48.280 --> 01:51:55.870
a two dimensional data set to a one dimensional&nbsp;
data set, but maintained some of the information&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:55.870 --> 01:52:00.610
in the data. But I like to think that we've&nbsp;
maintained most of the information. And hopefully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:00.610 --> 01:52:07.900
we maintain the most important information in&nbsp;
our data set. And the reason we're doing this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:07.900 --> 01:52:13.120
is we've made the analysis and interpretation&nbsp;
easier and more reliable. By going from something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:13.120 --> 01:52:17.590
that was more complex, two dimensional or higher&nbsp;
dimensions, down to something that's simpler to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:17.590 --> 01:52:23.770
deal with fewer dimensions, it means easier to&nbsp;
make sense of in general, let me show you how&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:23.770 --> 01:52:31.390
this works in our open up this script. And we'll&nbsp;
go through an example in our studio. To do this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

